{
  "id": "Compound::DB00860 - Disease::DOID:2377",
  "verbalization": "Prednisolone and Methylprednisolone, both glucocorticoids, are implicated in the treatment of Multiple Sclerosis through a shared biological pathway involving 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid and Therapeutic Glucocorticoid. The evidence suggests that Prednisolone may contribute to the development of complications such as Myocardial Rupture, Alkalosis Hypokalaemic, and Fat Embolism, which are mediated by Methylprednisolone. These conditions, in turn, may influence the progression of Multiple Sclerosis, though the direct causal link remains unclear. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role of glucocorticoids in modulating inflammatory processes, though the exact mechanism by which these effects translate to disease outcomes requires further investigation. The shared biochemical profile of Prednisolone and Methylprednisolone underscores their potential as therapeutic agents, but the specific mechanisms by which they interact with the central nervous system to influence Multiple Sclerosis remain areas of active research.",
